---
// The explanation below the Lab: the maze, the symbols, how Q-learning
// learns, exploring with ε, α and γ, and the model in short. Static HTML;
// formulas are rendered by KaTeX at build time.
import { renderTex } from '../../lib/katex';
import { equations } from './equations';

const tex = Object.fromEntries(equations.map((e) => [e.id, renderTex(e.tex)]));
---

<div class="explain">
  <section aria-labelledby="ql-data">
    <h2 id="ql-data">What you are looking at</h2>
    <p>
      A mouse starts in the bottom-left square of a maze. It can move up, right,
      down or left. Finding the cheese is worth +10, meeting the cat costs 10,
      and every step costs 1, so a short safe path is best. Nobody tells the
      mouse the way: it only feels the rewards.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each square is cut into four triangles, one for each move. A triangle
      turns green when that move looks good and orange when it looks bad. The
      arrow shows the best move from each square, and the dashed line is the
      path the mouse would take if it always picked its best move. The mazes are
      made up, and <strong>Refresh Data</strong> shows another one.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="ql-symbols">
    <h2 id="ql-symbols">What each symbol means</h2>
    <dl class="symbols">
      <div>
        <dt>s, a</dt>
        <dd>A square (the state) and a move from it (the action).</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>s′</dt>
        <dd>The square the move leads to.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>r</dt>
        <dd>The reward for the move: −1, +10 or −10.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Q(s, a)</dt>
        <dd>
          What the mouse thinks the move is worth: the reward it expects from
          here on.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="sym-a">α</dt>
        <dd>The learning rate: how far each update moves Q.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="sym-b">γ</dt>
        <dd>How much later rewards count compared with rewards now.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="sym-error">ε</dt>
        <dd>
          How often the mouse tries a random move instead of its best one.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="sym-line">n</dt>
        <dd>
          How many episodes the mouse has played. An episode ends at the cheese,
          at the cat, or after 100 moves.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="ql-how">
    <h2 id="ql-how">How the mouse learns</h2>
    <p>
      At first every Q is 0, so the mouse wanders. After each move it updates
      the value of the move it just made, using only what it saw: the reward,
      and the best value it already gives the square it landed on.
    </p>
    <div class="formula" set:html={tex.update} />
    <p>
      The part in brackets is the surprise: how much better or worse the move
      turned out than expected. Q moves a fraction α of the way towards it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The first time the mouse finds the cheese, only the last move learns that
      it is good. Next time, the move before it sees a square worth something,
      and learns that too. Episode by episode, the good news spreads backwards
      from the cheese to the start, until the arrows lead all the way there.
    </p>
    <div class="formula" set:html={tex.value} />
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="ql-explore">
    <h2 id="ql-explore">Exploring, and the other settings</h2>
    <p>
      If the mouse always took its best known move, it might keep using the
      first path it found and never discover a shorter one. So with chance ε it
      tries a random move instead. With ε = 0 it never explores; with a large ε
      it wanders so much that its runs are long and messy.
    </p>
    <p>
      A large α learns fast but forgets fast; a small α learns slowly but
      steadily. In this maze nothing is random except the mouse's own choices,
      so a large α works well. γ near 1 makes the mouse patient: the cheese
      still counts when it is many steps away. A small γ makes it short-sighted,
      and the good news from the cheese fades before it reaches the start.
    </p>
    <p>
      Click a square to turn it into a wall, then a cat, then back. The mouse
      learns the new maze from scratch, with the same luck as before.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="ql-summary">
    <h2 id="ql-summary">The model in short</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Act:</strong> pick the best known move, or with chance ε a
        random one.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Observe</strong> the reward and the next square.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Update</strong> Q for that move towards the reward plus γ times
        the best value of the next square.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Repeat</strong> over many episodes, until the best moves lead to
        the goal.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>
</div>

<style>
  .explain {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-12);
    max-width: 68ch;
    font-size: var(--fs-16);
    line-height: 1.8;
  }

  section {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-4);
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: var(--fs-24);
  }

  .formula {
    overflow-x: auto;
    padding-block: var(--s-2);
    font-size: var(--fs-18);
  }

  .symbols {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-3);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .symbols div {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 5rem minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--s-4);
    align-items: baseline;
  }

  .symbols dt {
    font-family: 'KaTeX_Math', 'Times New Roman', serif;
    font-size: var(--fs-20);
    font-style: italic;
  }

  .symbols dd {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .steps {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-3);
    margin: 0;
    padding-inline-start: 1.4em;
  }
</style>
